ITA Coverage Application Test Report
By Amogi Endurance Friday
Reviewed/Supervised by James Bach

Mission
The mission was to explore and evaluate the ITA Coverage Application and determine whether it correctly identifies establishments required to submit 300A and 300/301 form to OSHA injury and illness data. https://www.satisfice.com/osha.htm. This report describes the application behavior, the testing process, and issues identified during testing.
Product Information
The ITA Coverage Application is a decision-support tool developed under the Occupational Safety and Health Administration. Which helps establishment determine whether they are required to electronically submit 300A and 300/301 form through the ITA. The logic is based on; https://www.osha.gov/recordkeeping/presentations/exempttable 29 CFR Part 1904 OSHA Recordkeeping Regulation Specifically 29 CFR 1904.41 https://www.osha.gov/recordkeeping/naics-codes-electronic-submission and https://www.osha.gov/sites/default/files/appendix_b_to_subpart_e_of_part_1904.pdf
The following key input variables were identified as critical to the system’s decision logic:
•	Company size 
•	Industry classification (NAICS code) 
•	Employer type (Private or Government) 
•	Location (State (USA) selection)
Test Process
I began by exploring the application to identify the variables that influence reporting decisions before going into targeted tests around thresholds, classification behavior, employer type separation, and rule interaction.
The following activities were carried out during testing:
•	Interaction with all functional user interface elements, including dropdowns, input fields, and links navigation
•	Systematic variation of input combinations such as: 
•	Different employee size values 
•	Various industry classifications (NAICS codes) 
•	Employer type selections (Private, Federal Government, State/Local Government) 
•	Observation of system responses to determine decision logic accuracy 
Oracles Used
The expected results in this testing were derived from OSHA recordkeeping and reporting requirements under 1904.1(a)(1)(2) as implemented in the ITA Coverage Application. The following rules were used as the basis for determining expected system behavior:
Rule 1 – 250 Establishments (Form 300A)
Establishments with 250 or more employees, and not classified in exempt industries (Appendix A to Subpart B of 29 CFR Part 1904), are required to submit Form 300A data. 29 CFR 1904.41(a)(1)(ii)
Rule 2 – 20-249 Establishments (Form 300A)
Establishments with 20–249 employees, and operating in industries listed in Appendix A to Subpart E of 29 CFR Part 1904, are required to submit Form 300A data. 29 CFR 1904.41(a)(1)(i)
Rule 3 – Detailed Reporting Requirement 100 or more employees (Forms 300, 301, and 300A)
Establishments with 100 or more employees, and operating in industries listed in Appendix B to Subpart E of 29 CFR Part 1904, are required to submit Forms 300, 301, and 300A data. 29 CFR 1904.41(a)(2)
Rule 4 – Exempt or Low-Risk Establishments
Establishments that do not meet the required employee thresholds or are classified in partially exempt industries are not required to keep OSHA injury and illness records or submit data, unless specifically requested. 29 CFR 1904.41(b)(1) – Partial exemption for establishments in certain industries
•	Private sector employers are subject to ITA reporting requirements based on size and industry classification. 
•	Federal Agency reports Injury and Illness Information. 29 CFR 1960.72(a) Each agency must submit to the Secretary by May 1 of each year all information included on the agency’s previous calendar year’s occupational injury and illness recordkeeping forms. The information submitted must include all data entered on the OSHA Form 300, Log of Work-Related Injuries and Illnesses (or equivalent); OSHA Form 301, Injury and Illness Incident Report (or equivalent); and OSHA However, 491110 Postal Service is required to maintain OSHA injury and illness records (Forms 300, 300A, and 301) in accordance with 29 CFR Part 1904, as documented in the USPS Employee and Labor Relations Manual (ELM), Section 821.11.
•	State and local government employers in State Plan states have different requirements; in such cases, the system may return a conditional result advising users to consult their state agency. Source: https://www.osha.gov/stateplans
Test Strategy
The test design was based on OSHA regulatory rules and decision boundaries. Since there are about 1179 NAICS codes, and animation wasn’t used. I selected test values using a structured approach based on OSHA regulatory groupings and reporting logic.
The strategy focused on covering: (See Test coverage outline below)
•	OSHA NAICS regulatory groups (Appendix A, Appendix B, and exception cases) 
•	Employee count decision thresholds 
•	Employer type separation rules 
•	Interactions between employee count, NAICS classification, employer type, and state selection 
The goal was to verify whether the system correctly applies OSHA reporting rules across different regulatory scenarios.
The detailed test design approach is shown in the table below.
Test Area	How Test Values Were Selected	Why I Selected Them	What I Was Testing
NAICS Regulatory Grouping	I selected NAICS codes from OSHA Appendix A (partially exempt industries), Appendix B (100+ employee reporting industries), standard non-exempt industries, and USPS (491110) as a special exception case	I wanted the test design to follow OSHA regulatory groupings instead of general industry categories	Whether the system applies the correct OSHA reporting rules based on NAICS classification
Employee Count (Thresholds)	I used values: 9,10, 20, 50, 99, 100, 120, 250, 300, 500000, and 1000000	These values were chosen around OSHA rule thresholds (20, 100, and 250), including values below, above, and extremely high values	Whether the system changes reporting decisions correctly at threshold points and still behaves correctly with very large numbers
Employer Type Separation	I tested Private, Federal Government, and State/Local Government	OSHA applies different rules to each employer type	Whether the system keeps the rule logic separate for each employer type
State Selection (State Plan Logic)	I selected different U.S. states such as California, Oregon, Minnesota, Utah, and others	Some states operate under State Plans while others follow Federal OSHA rules	Whether state selection changes reporting results correctly or only provides State Plan guidance
Input Validation Testing	I entered invalid values such as -560, 100.5, 100A, and zero values	These inputs were chosen to test how the system handles incorrect or unusual data	Whether invalid inputs are rejected or accepted
Exception Handling (USPS Case)	I tested NAICS 491110 across different employee sizes and employer types	USPS is a known OSHA exception case with different handling requirements	Whether the system correctly applies exception logic instead of standard reporting rules

