Test Observations
During testing, the application was generally stable and easy to use.
1. Input Validation
The system correctly required all mandatory fields before submission. When fields were missing, it stopped the user from continuing.
It also correctly handled invalid inputs such as negative numbers, decimals (e.g. 100.5), letters mixed with numbers (e.g. 100A). These inputs were either rejected or flagged, but and scientific notation (e.g. 1e2) was accepted. This shows that basic input checking is working properly.
2. Employee Count Behaviour
The system correctly applied OSHA employee rules at key points like 20, 100, and 250 employees for private companies. Even very large values like 500,000 and 1,000,000 did not break the system, and it still returned results normally.
3. NAICS Classification Behaviour
Different NAICS codes produced different results in private-sector cases, which shows that industry type is used in decision making. However, results were not always consistent when comparing different employer types, especially federal cases.
4. USPS Exception
The only exception was USPS (491110), which worked correctly and followed expected rules.
5. State Selection Behaviour
When “State/Local Government” was selected, the system correctly showed “State Plan” guidance.
6. Combined Rules (Interaction Testing)
When inputs were used together (employee count, NAICS code, employer type, and state), the system generally worked well.
Key Risks
The primary risk identified is the delivery of incorrect regulatory guidance that appears valid, which may lead to improper compliance decisions.
•	The defect is consistent across multiple NAICS categories and employee thresholds, increasing the likelihood of widespread impact 
•	Inadequate NAICS validation may allow incorrect or unintended industry classifications, leading to inaccurate reporting determinations 
•	Acceptance of non-standard numeric formats (e.g., scientific notation) may introduce ambiguity or unintended inputs into decision logic 
•	Misinterpretation of regulatory requirements could result in under-reporting or over-reporting, creating potential compliance and operational risks 
•	The system may look like it’s working fine, but it can still give wrong results, and users might not notice unless they understand the rules well.
Recommendations
The following recommendations are based on observed inconsistencies in the application’s decision logic and validation behavior
•	Validate user input against NAICS codes to prevent acceptance of invalid or non-existent codes. 
•	It handles inputs such as scientific notation (e.g., 1e2) to ensure consistency with expected user input formats. 
•	Provide users with brief explanations of how decisions are made (e.g., based on employee size and industry classification) to improve trust and clarity. 
•	Implement checks to ensure that logic applied aligns with the selected employer type (Private, Federal, State/Local), preventing cross-application of rules. 
•	Ensure that similar inputs produce consistent results across different states and scenarios, especially for Federal and State Plan logic. 
•	Where applicable, enhance messaging for State/Local Government selections to guide users more precisely on next steps.
Conclusion 
Based on my assessment, the ITA Coverage Application is generally stable and behaves consistently across most tested scenarios. The system correctly handles employee thresholds, NAICS classification, required field validation, and State Plan routing. Testing showed that the application uses rule-based logic tied to OSHA reporting requirements. Federal government scenarios were more complex because federal agencies are also required to submit injury and illness information under 29 CFR 1960.72(a). Overall, the application appears functional and reliable, but stronger NAICS validation and clearer explanation of reporting decisions would improve confidence in the system’s results.









